<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishida Mitsunari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1560">1560</a>–<a href="/years/1600">1600</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishida Mitsunari was a Japanese warlord and administrator, one of the five commissioners who ran the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>. After Hideyoshi's death he organised the western coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>, which was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600. He was captured and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, and his lands at Sawayama were given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naomasa">Ii Naomasa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari.jpg" alt="Ishida Mitsunari" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ishida_Mitsunari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mitsunari was born in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>, in what is now Nagahama in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiga-prefecture">Shiga Prefecture</a>, the second son of Ishida Masatsugu, a retainer of the Azai clan. His childhood name was Sakichi. The Ishida withdrew from service after the Azai were defeated at the Siege of Odani Castle in 1573. A legend that he was a Buddhist monk before entering the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> is of doubtful accuracy, since it was first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. In 1577 he met Hideyoshi, then daimyō of Nagahama.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mitsunari assisted Hideyoshi at the sieges of Tottori Castle and Takamatsu Castle and fought at Shizugatake in 1583. In 1585 he became administrator of Sakai with his elder brother, and was one of the five bugyō, the top administrators of Hideyoshi's government, with Asano Nagamasa, Maeda Gen'i, Mashita Nagamori and Natsuka Masaie. He became daimyō of Sawayama and in 1588 took charge of Hideyoshi's sword hunt to disarm the non-military population. In 1590 he commanded the Siege of Oshi, where his plan to flood the castle failed; other records say Hideyoshi himself ordered it.</p><p>He was one of the Three Bureaucrats in the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> from 1592. In 1598 several generals, dissatisfied with his assessments of their achievements in Korea, marched on his mansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. He fled to Fushimi Castle, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> mediated. Some historians call the incident the generals' lawsuit against Mitsunari rather than a plot to kill him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the deaths of Hideyoshi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-toshiie">Maeda Toshiie</a> in 1599, Mitsunari formed an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> loyalists against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a>. His army was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> on 21 October 1600. Ōtani Yoshitsugu had warned him that the daimyō disliked him. Mitsunari was caught by villagers while trying to escape, beheaded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and his head displayed.</p><p>Stephen Turnbull writes that traditional historiography neglected him because he lost, often portraying him as a weak bureaucrat, and that later historians note his planning skill and think <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> could have gone his way had a few more lords stayed loyal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ishida Mitsunari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1600, in the name of Hideyoshi's young heir, he defeated the western coalition led by <b>Ishida Mitsunari</b> at the Battle of Sekigahara, one of the largest battles ever fought on Japanese soil, with perhaps 160,000 combatants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ishida Mitsunari</b>'s Western Army laid siege to Fushimi Castle, held for Tokugawa Ieyasu by a much smaller garrison under Torii Mototada.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ii Naomasa, one of Tokugawa Ieyasu's most trusted generals and leader of the red-armoured troops, was given the former lands of <b>Ishida Mitsunari</b> at Sawayama after Sekigahara in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/hikone-domain">Hikone Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ishida-chō. Died in Rokujō Riverbed. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
